# Bot Conventions

## Handler Naming

All Telegram handler functions follow the pattern `handle_<command>` or `handle_<intent>`:

```python
# ✅ GOOD
async def handle_start(update: Update, context: ContextTypes.DEFAULT_TYPE) -> None: ...
async def handle_weather(update: Update, context: ContextTypes.DEFAULT_TYPE) -> None: ...

# ❌ BAD
async def start(update, ctx): ...
async def weather_handler(update, ctx): ...
```

## Weather Formatting

All weather data sent to the user must go through `format_weather()` in `weather.py`. Never construct the reply string inline inside a handler:

```python
# ✅ GOOD
text = format_weather(data)
await update.message.reply_text(text)

# ❌ BAD
await update.message.reply_text(f"Temp: {data['main']['temp']}°C")
```

## Environment Variable Keys

Use exactly these keys in `.env` and `config.py`:

| Key | Purpose |
|-----|---------|
| `BOT_TOKEN` | Telegram Bot API token from @BotFather |
| `WEATHERBIT_API_KEY` | Weatherbit API key (weatherbit.io) |
| `WEBHOOK_URL` | Public HTTPS URL for webhook mode (e.g. `https://yourdomain.com`) |
| `PORT` | Port for the webhook ASGI server (default `8443`) |

## Module Responsibilities

- `config.py` — reads env vars, validates they are set, exports constants
- `weather.py` — all Weatherbit logic, `get_weather()` + `format_weather()`
- `handlers.py` — all PTB handler functions, imports from `weather.py`
- `bot.py` — polling entry point, imports from `handlers.py`
- `webhook_server.py` — webhook entry point, imports from `handlers.py`
